Okay, two new ideas for conducts (don't know whether they're feasible
or not, but I'll just throw them out and see what people think):

1) Packrat Conduct

As a packrat, you are a character who just can't bear to throw
anything away that's of any value at all. The consequence is that you
have to clean out the *entire dungeon* and take every object ever
created with you when you ascend -- either carrying it in your
inventory, wearing it, or having consumed or destroyed it (i.e. by
burning, eating, etc.)
Exceptions to this rule would include: rocks (there's just too many of
them), iron chains, and other (if any) objects with no monetary value;
lodestones; gold coins (this is covered by my second suggested
conduct); and, finally, objects that belong to shopkeepers. This means
that you can sell objects in return for gold, but can't go on dropping
objects in return for "credit" once the shopkeeper's store of money
has been exhausted (otherwise, you could dump all unwanted objects
inside a shop.) (In other words, if you have any credit when you
ascend, you do not satisfy packrat conduct, though temporary credit
could be allowable.)

2. Scrooge Conduct

Similar to the conduct above. This time you are a super-greedy
character who just can't bear to part with any gold. As a consequence,
when you ascend you must carry with you every piece of gold ever
created in the dungeon (perhaps gems as well). You can't donate to
priests (who ever heard of Scrooge giving away money?) but you can buy
things from shops. You must kill every gold-carrying monster you find
and take their gold with you when you ascend; this includes priests
and shopkeepers (except Izchak, of course.) You cannot polymorph into
a metallivore and eat the gold to get rid of it. In other words, every
single piece of gold ever generated must be with you when you ascend.

Any comments?

